WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.570
[Mosquito buzzing sound]

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.550
Malaria is an infectious disease caused by
a parasite called Plasmodium.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.640
It is spread from person to person by infected
Anopheles mosquitoes.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.199
The parasite’s lifecycle is complex.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:36.650
When an infected mosquito feeds on a human it injects
parasites, known as sporozoites, into the blood.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.930
These parasites travel to the liver where
they develop into merozoites.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:49.140
These are released into the blood where they
invade red blood cells and multiply many times.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.140
They then burst out, destroying the red blood
cells, leading to the symptoms of malaria

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:58.130
such as anaemia and fever.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:03.190
Inside some red blood cells the parasite develops
into male and female gametocytes.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.190
These are taken up by another mosquito when
it feeds on the blood.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
The gametocytes mature in the mosquito gut
and then fuse together to produce a new parasite.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.780
This develops into thousands of sporozoites
that are injected into a human

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:29.780
when the mosquito feeds and so the cycle starts
again.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.570
Malaria affects millions of people around
the world. Today malaria infections are restricted

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:42.890
to some of the world’s poorest countries,
in tropical regions such as Southern Asia,

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:49.890
Africa and South America. However, if you
go back just one hundred years malaria was

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:55.060
found in most parts of the world including
Europe and the United States of America.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.170
“Malaria used to be a big problem in Europe,
all the way up into England. It used to be

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.689
a problem in the United States, Washington
DC was a terribly malarious area and even

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:09.920
up into New England, New York, Massachusetts,
there was a lot of malaria. It’s now completely

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.700
gone so we can eliminate malaria.”

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.390
A simple way to reduce the transmission of
malaria is with the use of insecticide-treated

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:23.069
bed nets. These act as a barrier and protect
people from mosquito bites at night as they

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:25.030
sleep.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.420
Nets can be supplied to communities and training
can be given to make sure that people know

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.640
how to use them properly. However distribution
can be a problem and many people still get

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:42.640
infected with malaria.There are over 200 million
cases of malaria reported every year. 

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.120
The main symptoms of the disease are a high fever
and anaemia but severe cases can lead to life

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.209
threatening complications.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:59.079
Over 780,000 lives are lost each year because
of malaria. Children under the age of five

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:05.530
are most at risk and on average a child dies
of malaria every 30 seconds.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.450
The disease can also cause extreme complications
in pregnant women, leading to the death of

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.560
200,000 newborn babies each year.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.379
“So pregnant women can get massive malaria
infections, specifically in their placenta

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:24.890
and that causes reduced oxygen and nutrient
uptake to the developing foetus, which can

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:30.709
result in premature birth, so you get babies
delivered early with a low birth rate, or

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:36.190
even still birth and abortion can be caused
by these placental malarial infections.”

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:40.800
Despite the potential severity of malaria
it can be easily treated with drugs that kill

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.400
the parasite. The use of simple blood tests
enables the early detection and diagnosis

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:53.010
of the disease. Antimalarial drugs can then
be given to shorten the duration of the disease

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:59.650
and prevent further complications and death.
A continual challenge to treating malaria

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:05.909
is the emergence of drug resistant parasites.
This happened with the drug chloroquine developed

00:04:05.909 --> 00:04:07.129
in the 1940s.

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:14.129
“In the case of chloroquine historically
we knew that resistance had first come up

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:19.709
in South East Asia, right on the Thailand-Cambodian
border in the late 1950s. Right around the

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:25.080
same time it was reported in the Brazilian
Amazon and then once we figured out what was

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.889
the genetic mutation that caused that resistance
we were able to determine that, in fact, those

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:35.190
had been independent events. The form that
came up in South East Asia then spread throughout

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:41.560
the rest of Asia and by 1978 it had arrived
in Africa and then spread over the next 10

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.530
or 15 years through Africa.”

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:50.300
In the 1990s a new drug called artemisinin
was developed, but within 10 years parasites

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:56.240
resistant to this drug emerged in South East
Asia. To minimize the chances of resistance

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:01.660
developing in the parasite malaria drugs are
now used in combinations rather than as single

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.600
drug treatments.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:09.600
“So there’s a lot of anxiety in the malaria
world right now about our current first line

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:17.240
class of drugs called the artemisinins because
a sort of a mild form of resistance has emerged

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:21.759
in the last few years and looking at what
happened with chloroquine, looking at what

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:27.410
happened with the anti-folate drugs, the fear
is that it’s also going to spread from South

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:32.220
East Asia eventually to Africa and right now
there’s really nothing in the drug development

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.990
pipeline that would be able to replace it.
So if we lose the artemisinins we’re in

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.110
big trouble.”

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.600
By looking at the different stages of the
parasite’s lifecycle it is hoped that targets

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:49.060
for new drugs and vaccines can be identified.
Genome research is at the forefront of these investigations.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.710
“A genome, when it comes down to it, is
a list of all the genes that are present in

00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:00.050
an organism, so the human genome gives us
the list of everything that it takes to make

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:05.729
a human. In the same way, a parasite genome
sequence, gives us a list of all the genes

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:12.050
it takes to make a parasite. So to a malaria
researcher that genome sequence is a list

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:16.240
of targets, it’s a list of things that we
can develop drugs against, a list of things

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.350
that we can develop vaccines against and if
you have many hundreds of different genomes

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:25.900
you can see which of those genes are variable,
which of those genes are conserved, which

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:30.690
of them are essential, which of them some
parasites have and some parasites don’t.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:36.250
All of that information is hugely beneficial
to our efforts to develop new drugs or vaccines

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.620
against malaria.”

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.820
There is lots of research taking place around
the world to improve our understanding of

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:48.229
the biology of malaria. This research is looking
at the parasite, the mosquito vector and the

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:54.470
human host. It is hoped that this will lead
to new interventions for the prevention, treatment

00:06:54.470 --> 00:07:00.120
and ultimately the eradication of malaria,
saving millions of lives worldwide.

